        "titulo": "Word order and the first questions",
        "explicacao": "STEP 1: listen to «Ich heiße Lea.» and then «Wie heißt du?». To say your name, the verb is «heißen», which means to be called. STEP 2: in German, the conjugated verb always stays in the SECOND position of the sentence. In «Ich heiße Lea», the «ich» takes the first position, and «heiße» takes the second. STEP 3: in questions with a question word, that word takes the first place. Examples: «wie», which means how and reads «vee», and «woher», which means where from and reads «voh-HAIR». The verb stays second: «Wie heißt du?» and «Woher kommst du?». STEP 4: in a yes/no question, the verb goes to the front: «Heißt du Lea?». German needs no «do» here. Answer «Ja» or «Nein». STEP 5: the verb changes its ending according to the person. With «ich», it ends in «e»: «ich heiße», «ich komme». With «du», it ends in «st»: «du kommst». In «heißen», the «ß» already makes an «s» sound, so you add only the «t»: «du heißt». STEP 6: do not forget the pronoun. German, like English, needs it: «Ich komme aus Brasilien», never just «Komme aus Brasilien». STEP 7: a warning. In the words «der Name» and «der Vorname», the «der» is an article. You learn articles in lesson 3. For now, repeat the «der» together with the word.",
        "comparacao": "In English the verb also comes right after the subject, and you cannot drop the «I» either. The difference is that German fixes the verb in the second position even when another word opens the sentence, so the subject can end up after the verb (like «Today come I»).",
